## Triceratops

A large **ceratopsian herbivore** with two brow horns, a nose horn, and a solid frill.

{#0F766E}It really did live alongside *T. rex*.{/}

## Small and feathered

Real *Velociraptor* was about **2 m long**, lightly built, and lived in Mongolia.

{#C62828}The movies enlarged it and omitted its feathers.{/}

## Spinosaurus

A giant North African **spinosaurid** with conical teeth and a sail. Fish were important prey.

{#1A3A6B}Scientists still debate whether it pursued prey underwater or hunted mainly in shallows and on land.{/}

## Apatosaurus

A heavy-built **diplodocid sauropod** from North America.

{#1A3A6B}*Brontosaurus* was long treated as the same genus; many modern studies again recognize it separately.{/}

## Argentinosaurus

A giant **titanosaur** from Argentina, perhaps around **30–35 m** long.

{#1A3A6B}Because the skeleton is fragmentary, estimates vary widely.{/}

## Dilophosaurus

An Early Jurassic predator from North America, about **7 m** long, with paired head crests.

{#C62828}The movie frill, venom, and tiny size are inventions.{/}

## Leaellynasaura

A small **ornithopod** from Early Cretaceous Australia. It endured months of winter darkness in a cool polar forest.

{#1A3A6B}Its exact anatomy is still being revised.{/}

## Procompsognathus

A small Late Triassic animal from Germany.

{#1A3A6B}Fragmentary fossils make both its anatomy and precise classification uncertain.{/}

## Megaraptor

A large Cretaceous predator from Argentina with an elongated skull and huge **hand claws**.

{#C62828}Its famous claw was not a sickle claw from the foot.{/}

## Tyrannosaurus rex

Birds are **theropods**, as was *T. rex*. *Triceratops* belonged to the separate ornithischian branch.

{#0F766E}Birds are the only living dinosaurs.{/}

## Cretaceous

Approximately **145–66 million years ago**—longer than either the Triassic or Jurassic.

{#1A3A6B}Its name does not mean dinosaurs lived in it for only a brief final chapter.{/}

## Gripping prey

Biomechanical comparisons suggest the raised second-toe claw helped **hook and restrain** prey.

{#1A3A6B}Its exact use probably varied with body size and prey.{/}

## No

Pterosaurs were flying **archosaurs**, not members of Dinosauria.

{#C62828}“Pterodactyl” is an informal label, not a synonym for dinosaur.{/}

## No

Bonebeds and trackways can suggest association, but **coordinated pack hunting** requires stronger evidence.

{#1A3A6B}Social behaviour is among the hardest dinosaur traits to reconstruct.{/}

## Fossils are incomplete

Researchers often estimate missing bones from close relatives and model body mass from limb dimensions or 3D volume.

{#0F766E}A range is usually more honest than a single exact number.{/}
